my teamwork with ai ni?

I had finished my lexer when I found her as a teammate, and I initially
tasked her with making the parser.

I finished my part and realised: I would need to see the parser to really
continue without guessing details
>>> like, where exactly do I have tokens, or, strings instead of the tokens
	is there a new struct involved
	is there any new contextual data being passed around?
	did she find a new behaviour (i.e. >a)

So, I went about making my own parser; it is much easier to describe my
thoughts on the task if I have my own examples.

That... went not so well. It turns out this is the first coding group
project for the both of us. We went our separate ways with the
parser.

In hindsight, despite our different approaches, the only problem I had was
that she converted the tokens back to strings too early.

At some point, I had gotten stuck dealing with brackets, as they are meant
to give some operations priority like in math statements. What prompted me
to go back and clear up the disagreements was that she had solved this
problem in her implementation. That's great, I'd use her parser. But my own
concerns were still there. So, I sat down with her again.

Two details were not clear on her end, and that is my fault.
One, she thought the system would resolve globs (asterisks) for us. Yes, there is a standard lib function for it, but using it was outside the project description.
The other, how env substitution and quotes work. Messy, messy details such
as how it interacts with globstars, and how bash splits a quotes and envs
into a different number of strings by itself; can be more, can be less, can be the same number but the propertions are shifted around.

I am an introvert, I naturally gravitate towards doing things myself. I
learned here I should focus on teamwork, on making sure the goals and
concerns are defined well.

After that, I worked on adjusting her parser to suit me, while she took a
break overseas. When she came back, I showed her the idea of how the
tokens would be converted to strings: we had to handle globs and envs
simultanteosly. I had tested and recorded the desirable behaviour this
time, so we did not clash again.

afterwards we had to do a few builtin commands, we casually claimed what
